== block 3: Theorem I1 (Theorem 7.4(a))
  the bound is below 1 exactly if n > K ln 2/ln(1/(1 - gamma/(2L))); on a grid of 252 triples (L, gamma, K)
    this threshold lies between (2L/gamma - 1) K ln 2 and (2L/gamma) K ln 2: its ratio to the first is between 1.0000 and 1.4427, to the second between 0.7213 and 1.0000
    example L = 1, gamma = 1: threshold K, against (2L/gamma - 1) K ln 2 = 0.693 K and (2L/gamma) K ln 2 = 1.386 K
  n >= (2L/gamma)(K ln 2 + ln(1/delta)) rounds make the bound at most delta: 0 violations on a grid of 108 cases
  exact enumeration on 60 random instances (L <= 3, scaffolds whose inputs depend on the answers, runs of unequal length, 4 to 7 functions, total weight 1 or 1/2):
    one round started from each of 1682 surviving sets: E[weight left on the members whose runs differ] <= (1 - gamma/(2L)) times that weight in every case; largest ratio of the two sides 0.9565
    after n = 1, ..., 12 rounds: expected posterior weight of those members <= 2^K (1 - gamma/(2L))^n in every case; largest ratio 0.2167
  an example with three functions (weights 0.01 for the human f, 0.98 for g, 0.01 for h; L = 2; both g and h at run distance 1):
    the member h is refuted in the first round with probability 0.0100, far below gamma/(2L) = 0.2500;
    the expected weight removed from g and h together is 0.4901 >= gamma/(2L) times their weight = 0.2475
